      "dom_id": "question-11931",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+1)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,u=3x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
