      "dom_id": "question-107472",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x+6)$ at the point $(1,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the slope of the tangent line. The slope is equal to the derivative of the curve at the given point:\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1}\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we first express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{log}⁡(u),\\,u=4x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(4x+6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(2(1)+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we know that the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,1)$ is $m=\\frac{2}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}.$ Finally, we find the equation of the tangent line at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{2}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2x}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}-\\frac{2}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
